I’ll first inspect the pattern metadata, the frozen baseline workflow, and the available diffraction utilities. Then I’ll build a chemically screened phase inventory, test competing records under a common profile model, and carry the accepted model through GSAS-II refinement and export.

The chemistry is SrCO₃ + WO₃ at 900 °C, so the initial inventory includes scheelite SrWO₄, residual precursor/decomposition phases, and plausible Sr–W–O intermediates or off-stoichiometric products. I’m now extracting the observed peak families and querying the frozen COD index rather than assuming the nominal 1:1 product is the only phase.

The scheelite SrWO₄ family is unequivocal: its isolated reflections recur from 18° through 95°, with the angle-dependent displacement expected from a slightly different cell plus specimen-height correction. A monoclinic WO₃-like triplet near 23.5–24.7° is also plausible, but a substantial set of coherent reflections remains; I’m indexing that residual set and screening off-stoichiometric Sr–W–O prototype families before accepting any minor phase.

The first one-phase refinement confirms that the extra families are real, not background: SrWO₄ accounts for the dominant repeated pattern but leaves organized residuals across the entire scan, and forcing a single common width drives profile terms to bounds. I’m now comparing physical alternate records under the same restrained baseline; the batch is still converging.

Several alternate fits are numerically unstable when their cells are released—useful negative evidence in itself, but I’m not letting those failures contaminate the baseline. I’m retaining their fixed-cell scale tests separately and will only release a candidate cell after its reflection families align coherently.

The residual index points to a distorted double-perovskite-type Sr₃WO₆ phase. In particular, an Sr₃UO₆ parent transformed by U→W predicts the otherwise unexplained 20.7°, 29.5–30.0°, 35.0°, 42.1°, 61.1–62.3°, and 69–70° families. I’ve generated several symmetry-preserving Sr₃WO₆ analogues and am testing them alongside scheelite SrWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃; the defective Ba-parent record was rejected for a 0.68 Å contact.

The candidate survives the common-profile challenge in both P2₁ and centrosymmetric P2₁/c forms. I’m now separating crystallite-size and microstrain contributions phase by phase; this is the key test for whether the apparent Sr₃WO₆ improvement is structural or merely a peak-width surrogate.

The phase-width refinements are computationally heavier than the inventory screens because each stage is replaying the full 10–100° grid. They are still progressing; no additional nuisance terms are being introduced while this comparison runs.

The residual-led search identifies Sr₂WO₅ much more strongly than Sr₃WO₆. A Pnma Ba₂ReO₅ prototype with Ba→Sr and Re→W, after physically expected cell contraction, cuts the weighted residual sum by about 54% on its own and accounts for the two unique low-angle peaks as well as the 29.75°, 30.91°, 32.60°, and 44.65° families. I’m promoting Sr₂WO₅ into the provisional inventory and returning Sr₃WO₆ to the watch list.

The three-phase inventory now gives Rwp ≈10.9% even with an intentionally crude common width, and Sr₂WO₅ refines to about 25 wt%. I’m comparing restrained common profiles with phase-specific size only; this should lower the mismatch without allowing instrument width to impersonate a missing phase.

The three-phase inventory is now coherent: scheelite SrWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and a Pnma Sr₂WO₅ analogue derived from the Ba₂ReO₅ prototype. I’ve rejected Sr₃WO₆ and other plausible records by replayed residual fits; I’m now tightening the accepted model with cautious coordinate/texture tests, then I’ll export and independently replay the exact final project.

The first coordinate trial is not convincing: scheelite oxygen motion changes Rwp by only ~0.06 percentage point and remains poorly converged, so I will not accept it. The more flexible WO₃ and Sr₂WO₅ trials are still being checked for convergence and physically sensible contacts before any coordinate change is retained.

The broad coordinate refinements exposed an important confounder: they can lower Rwp by distorting bond geometry or by absorbing intensity systematics. I’m therefore rolling back those provisional coordinate changes and challenging the symmetry-distinct March–Dollase texture axes under the same accepted roughness/profile model; only afterward will I revisit small, physically bounded coordinate shifts.

Texture is unsupported: all tested axes refined essentially to unity (dominant SrWO₄ within 0.7%, WO₃ within 1.2%, Sr₂WO₅ within 7.3%) and changed Rwp by at most 0.02 percentage point, so I’ve pruned it. A physically screened refinement of only the Sr/W coordinates in the analogue phase is supported and preserves a 1.77 Å shortest W–O contact; oxygen-coordinate trials in the other phases are being rejected as unstable or marginal. Next I’m rebuilding the common peak profile with sizes temporarily frozen, including a direct asymmetry test.

The common-profile challenge behaved cleanly: freeing U/W/X drove all three terms to physical bounds and froze them, so that model is rejected despite its lower numerical Rwp. SH/L alone converged stably to 0.0238 and is retained. Both uniaxial strain and uniaxial-size challenges were unsupported (strain collapsed/singular; size changed Rwp by only 0.02), so the final peak model remains common U/W/X plus isotropic phase sizes. I’m now completing the required parent-prototype comparison for Sr₂WO₅ before freezing the inventory.

The alternative Pnma A₂BO₅ parents have now been geometry-screened. Their least-strained axis permutations still create 1.19–1.27 Å contacts after mapping to the indexed Sr₂WO₅ cell, versus 1.77 Å for the Ba₂ReO₅-derived model. I’m finishing their fixed-cell scale tests anyway; this prevents rejecting a chemically plausible parent solely because its first structural mapping is imperfect.

The prototype comparison is decisive: under the same fixed indexed cell and nuisance model, the Nd₂TiO₅- and La₂TiO₅-derived alternatives refine the Sr₂WO₅ scale to effectively zero; the Eu analogue becomes singular. The Ba₂ReO₅-derived parent retains ~26.6 wt% and a positive scale. I’m freezing that inventory now and performing the final save–reopen replay and exact CIF/profile exports.

One caveat surfaced in the physical audit: the best-fitting 1960 WO₃ record contains four 1.58 Å O–O contacts. That is not a hard failure, but it makes the detailed oxygen geometry uncertain. I’m running the modern monoclinic WO₃ alternate through the same three-phase profile before deciding whether the modest fit advantage justifies retaining the older record.

The modern WO₃ record is the better choice: it passes the contact screen and, under the same three-phase size/roughness model, gives Rwp 8.376% versus 8.412% for the older record. I’m replacing the provisional WO₃ structure and replaying the already accepted Sr₂WO₅ coordinate, profile, and Uiso stages before regenerating the deliverables.

The modern WO₃ model remains preferred after the full replay: Rwp reaches 7.550%, all contacts pass, and a further W-coordinate trial gains only 0.014 percentage point with >95% coordinate correlation, so it is rejected. I’m now overwriting the provisional exports with this physically cleaner final model and repeating the independent atomic replay.

The structural model is converged and independently replayed. I’m doing the final provenance/geometry checks now, then I’ll add the brief uncertainty report and verify that every submitted artifact agrees with `final.gpx`.

The phase inventory survived the common-profile re-challenge: the minor WO₃ record is physically sound and fits slightly better than its older alternative, while the Sr₂WO₅ analogue is supported by several distinct peak families and does not collapse to zero scale. I’m recording the key limitations explicitly—especially that Sr₂WO₅’s oxygen substructure is prototype-derived.

Final validation passed: all three fractions sum to 1, the 8,282 rows in `fit.csv` match the profile stored in `final.gpx` exactly, the reported Rwp matches the project, no parameters remain frozen, and the independent replay reports no profile-review flags.

Three crystalline phases were resolved; fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline material:

| Phase | Space group | Weight fraction |
|---|---|---:|
| SrWO₄ | I4₁/a | 64.31% |
| WO₃ | P2₁/c | 9.60% |
| Sr₂WO₅ | Pnma | 26.09% |

Final fit: Rwp 7.5494%, Rp 5.6338%, GOF 2.1384. Sr₂WO₅ is supported by multiple peak families, but its oxygen substructure is analogue-derived and not uniquely determined. Amorphous content cannot be quantified without an internal standard.

